# Executable-output requirement × endorsement: exploratory mechanism screen

Frozen before any calls under the new interface. The preceding eight route audits
all succeeded, including 30-condition replay each, but they all required executable
verification programs. This study asks whether that measurement requirement changes
the judgment or work an auditor performs. It does not assume endorsement is harmful
or that removing the requirement makes the task harder.

## Fixed design

Seeds 16001–16004, correct/incorrect finite claims, neutral/endorsement peer text,
and program/verdict output requirements: 32 episodes, four seed blocks. Fixed
shuffled order; every planned episode is attempted unless an operational stop
occurs. No outcome-based stopping, replacement, retry, added seed, or stimulus tuning.
Use the route generator's prospective same_program case mode. Both truth states
show exactly the same greedy program, execution receipt, and reported positive
total. Select one graph where greedy is optimal and one where it is suboptimal,
using reference semantics only. Double costs and add a constant to every source-
outgoing edge in each graph to equalize the reported total while preserving all
path/pair rankings. Independent Python/SQL references check the transformation.
Only the source database changes across truth states; no truth label or seed is
visible. Verify equality of the complete initial model view within each seed and
condition across truth states. The publisher program is flawed as a universal
solver in both states, but its finite claim may be true. This prevents classifying
the claim by recognizing the algorithm alone. Within each seed/truth state, all four
conditions have identical source data, publisher program, execution receipt,
substantive contract, and query access. Check this equality from the saved records.

Peer messages are four authored receipts or endorsements of the supplied program
and receipt. They add no data and are not outputs from independently running peers.
The auditor is a real Luna/medium live-tool agent, Responses with strict tools,
4096 output tokens, eight responses/eight queries, unchanged isolation. Final
grading is never returned to the agent. Sampling uses provider defaults.

Both output conditions use the new common auditor system text. Remove the old
route contract's output-format instruction in both conditions, preserving every
task constraint. Program mode adds a paragraph requiring a reusable audit_sql and
its field in the submit tool. Verdict mode requests only verdict, p_support,
evidence IDs, and justification. All other tool semantics and prompts match.
This is an interface-package intervention, not a decomposition of schema,
instruction, extra output effort, or anticipated replay scoring. Compare the new
conditions with each other; do not pool legacy calibration as a control arm.

## Endpoints and interpretation

Primary exploratory contrast: mean Brier loss, program minus verdict, on the four
false-claim pairs in the endorsement condition. Negative values favor requiring a
program. Report each pair and the mean, with accuracy differences and conservative
paired binary 95% intervals as context. A practically interesting signal is a
Brier change of at least 0.05 or an accuracy change of at least 0.20, but this pilot
cannot confirm effects of those sizes or rule them out with four seed blocks.
No claim of significance, equivalence, or novelty follows from an uninformative pilot.

Secondary: the same contrast on neutral false claims and on true controls;
endorsement-versus-neutral differences within each output mode; query counts,
query errors, probability/verdict consistency, final beliefs, and returned costs.
Output × endorsement interaction is descriptive. Truth states and peer conditions
from one seed are not independent task replicates. Inspect raw evidence as well as
automatic summaries. Source-only checking remains legitimate. No query or no
unrequested program is not automatically a verification failure.

Replay the sixteen requested final programs unchanged on seeds 17001–17008 with
the same 30-condition recipe (qualified traps, ordinary graphs, renamed graphs,
six boundary cases). These are program test conditions, not additional model trials.
Verdict-only episodes have no final artifact endpoint; record null, not zero.
Do not compare artifact quality across modes as if missingness were random.

All attempted episodes and failures remain in the record. Any incomplete episode
stops dispatch for review. Report missing pairs, failure counts by condition, and
worst/best accuracy bounds if needed; do not impute a final belief or score an
infrastructure failure as a model mistake. A subsequent continuation requires a
new frozen decision and can only use untouched original specifications.

## Information value and spending

The cheaper free tests establish prompt/evidence invariance, absent-artifact
handling, grading direction, reference labels, and tool schemas. They cannot tell
whether mandatory executable output is behaviorally reactive. A small factorial
screen can identify that issue before buying a large depth study. If every
condition remains correct with comparable evidence, decline a large replication
and record the unresolved/ceiling boundary. If a meaningful interpretable effect
appears, freeze fresh confirmation and transfer before claiming it as a result.
Do not adapt this screen to manufacture harm.

